   "source": [
    "**Residuals.** For $\\lambda = 0$ the residual follows the theoretical rate: $1/\\rho_1 = 0.694$ predicted and $0.658$ measured for the Gaussian clusters, and $0.949$ predicted and $0.899$ measured for the moons. The measured rates are a bit faster, since the Chebyshev bound only uses $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$ and $\\lambda_n$, not the rest of the spectrum. The residual reaches $10^{-10}$ after 56 steps for the Gaussian clusters and 138 steps for the moons. Shifted power iteration needed about 1 230 and 48 000 iterations (= matrix–vector products) for the same eigenvalue, since its rate $r = 0.982$ is much closer to 1 than $1/\\rho_1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Pairs are labelled by the eigenvalue they approximate, not by their position among the sorted Ritz values: for each distinct eigenvalue we take the smallest residual among the Ritz values within half the distance to the neighbouring eigenvalues. A curve starts once a Ritz value has entered this window (e.g. only at $m = 132$ for $0.0346$ in the moons, whose neighbour $0.0351$ is just $0.0006$ away). Numbered by sorted position instead, the curves for the 2nd and 3rd pair had large spikes that were only an indexing effect: whenever a new copy of the eigenvalue $0$ moves down through the spectrum (see the next plot), it is temporarily the 2nd or 3rd smallest Ritz value, and the residual shown is that of the unconverged newcomer, while the converged pair for $0.619$ is still there.\n",
    "\n",
    "The bumps that remain on the curve for $\\lambda = 0$ (blobs $m \\approx 95, 130, 155$, moons $m \\approx 165, 190$) are real. Since the curve already takes the best residual among all Ritz values near $0$, a bump means that no Ritz vector near $0$ has a small residual at that step. This happens when a new copy arrives at $0$: $T_m$ then has two almost equal eigenvalues, whose eigenvectors are poorly determined, so both Ritz vectors are mixtures of the converged and the new direction. It is a consequence of the multiplicity of $0$ combined with rounding errors, and the same bumps show up in the eigenvector error below. The bump at $m \\approx 95$ (up to $10^{-8}$) lies far above the rounding level $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{mach}}\\lambda_n \\approx 4 \\cdot 10^{-15}$; the later ones are smaller. Below the rounding level the residual estimate $|\\beta_m e_m^T y^{(m)}|$ is no longer meaningful.\n",
    "\n",
    "The eigenvalues $0.619$ and $0.670$ converge more slowly than $0$ (residual $< 10^{-10}$ after 90 and 92 steps; moons $0.013$ and $0.0346$ after 143 and 147), because they lie close together with $0.695$ (and $0.0346$, $0.0351$ for the moons)."
   ]
