    "**Ritz values.** Each Ritz value decreases towards an eigenvalue of $L$ from above, and the end of the spectrum converges first: the isolated eigenvalue $0$ is found within about 10 steps, the clustered eigenvalues $0.619, 0.670, 0.695$ need 50–60 steps. Although $0$ has multiplicity 4 (blobs) and 3 (moons), in exact arithmetic Lanczos would find it only once. In floating point, rounding errors bring in the other directions of the null space, and after many steps further copies of $0$ appear (the single points falling to $0$; a new Ritz value below $10^{-8}$ appears at $m = 90, 125, 150$ for the blobs and $m = 157, 184$ for the moons). After 200 steps there are 4 Ritz values below $10^{-8}$ for the blobs and 3 for the moons, matching the multiplicities, but the Ritz vectors from these late copies are not a reliable basis of the null space, which is why clustering with them does not reproduce the Part 1 clustering (see `test_lanczos.py`)."
