   "source": [
    "**Size.** The number of steps grows slowly with $N$: from 69 to 238 for the smallest pair and from 71 to 480 for the 4 smallest pairs, for $N = 200$ to $6400$. Shifted power iteration with deflation (`handin.ipynb`) needed 2 851 to 21 314 matrix–vector products for $N \\le 1600$, and did not converge within 50 000 iterations per eigenpair for $N \\geq 3200$. While the four clusters are separate ($m_0 = 4$, $N \\le 1600$), the prediction from $\\rho_1$ has the right size (52–143 steps against 56–109 observed). For $N \\geq 3200$ the clusters touch ($m_0 < 4$) and the next eigenvalue is tiny ($0.0024$, $0.0014$). Then the bound predicts 1 100–1 500 steps, but Lanczos needs only 211 and 238. This is the *superlinear* convergence of Lanczos: the bound only uses $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$ and $\\lambda_n$, while the Krylov space adapts to the whole spectrum. Once Ritz values have converged to the few small eigenvalues near $0$, the remaining convergence is governed by the larger gap to the rest of the spectrum.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sparsity.** With more neighbours $\\lambda_n$ grows ($12 \\to 82$), but for $k \\le 40$ the gap grows faster ($0.12 \\to 11.2$), so $1/\\rho_1$ falls from $0.82$ to $0.44$ and the smallest pair needs 90 down to 30 steps, in line with the prediction. For $k \\geq 80$ the graph connects the clusters ($m_0 = 3$, then $1$) and the gap drops to about $0.02$, so the bound predicts about 800 steps. Lanczos still needs only 27–34 steps, again because there are only a few small eigenvalues, separated from the rest of the spectrum. For power iteration the rate $r = (c - \\lambda_{\\text{next}})/(c - \\lambda_j)$ is fixed by a single gap, which is why it slows down here and Lanczos does not.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Four pairs.** `m_K` counts the steps until the 4 smallest *Ritz* pairs have converged. When $0$ has multiplicity $m_0 > 1$, these are $0$ (once) and the next distinct eigenvalues ($\\lambda_5, \\lambda_6, \\lambda_7$ for $m_0 = 4$), not $m_0$ eigenvectors of $0$. So `m_K` measures convergence, not whether the Ritz vectors can be used for clustering (see the Ritz value plot above).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cost.** Each step of Algorithm 6.15 costs one sparse matrix–vector product, $O(\\mathrm{nnz})$, plus $O(N)$ vector operations. Full reorthogonalization adds $O(Nm)$ in step $m$. The measured time per step grows with nnz$(L)$, although for the small matrices it is dominated by Python overhead (a few $10^{-5}$ s). For $N = 6400$ reorthogonalization made a step 2.5–7 times more expensive over the first 100 steps (in different runs), and the share grows with $m$. Our Lanczos converges in about 0.005–1.6 s, while `eigsh` needs 0.003–0.12 s: ARPACK uses the same Krylov idea (implicitly restarted Lanczos) in compiled code, with restarts that keep the basis small. The timings vary a bit between runs."
